# --- registry -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by setup/lib/models.sh; passed to llama-server as environment and
# ignored there.
#
# THIS PROFILE WAS WRITTEN IN AUGUST 2026 AND MEASURED FOR THE FIRST TIME ON
# 04.09.2026. Until that day it had never been through bench/speed.py — no
# report existed for it — and it carried six flags nobody had checked, four of
# which were wrong. What replaced them is below, each beside the measurement
# that justifies it. The full account:
#   bench/reports/2026-09-04_1810_gemma26-memory/       memory, seven loads
#   bench/reports/2026-09-04_1823_sweep_gemma26/        backend x speculation
#   bench/reports/2026-09-04_1905_gemma26-vs-qwen36/    the verdict
MODEL_TITLE=Gemma 4 26B-A4B · QAT-q4_0, 13.5 GiB · Vulkan + Google's own 0.30 GiB draft model · 17.0 GiB GTT at -c 32768 · prose 91.5/65.9/49.8 t/s, 1.5-2.1x production qwen36 — FAST AND CHEAP, AND CAPPED AT 32k BECAUSE IT LOSES THE MIDDLE OF A LONG CONTEXT: depth-correctness fails from 51,728 tokens in FOUR configurations while qwen36 and glm47flash are 48/48 clean (04.09.2026)
# MODEL_SWA=yes — read out of the GGUF: gemma4.attention.sliding_window = 1024,
# and sliding_window_pattern marks 25 of 30 layers sliding.
#
# THE USUAL CONSEQUENCE OF THIS LINE DOES NOT HOLD HERE, AND IT IS MEASURED.
# The rule elsewhere in this repo is "MODEL_SWA=yes means the profile MUST
# carry --swa-full". This profile does not carry it, and on 04.09.2026 that
# became a measured decision rather than the gap it had been. See --swa-full
# under the arguments below: it is worth 72 tokens and 0.4 s here, and costs
# 24.96 GiB. `tests/test_models.py`'s KNOWN_MISSING still lists gemma26, and
# now for a reason.
MODEL_SWA=yes
# MODEL_SOURCE — where this model comes from, so the profile can fetch it.
# `<repo> <pattern>…`: every pattern is a substring of a path in that repo and
# every matching .gguf is fetched, so a sharded quant comes down whole and an
# mmproj rides along. Verified 26.08. by matching the exact filename in the
# repo, not by guessing.
#
# NOTE THE mmproj PATTERN IS NOT THE PUBLISHED FILENAME. Upstream calls it
# `mmproj-F16.gguf`, which two models in this directory would both claim — the
# model directory is FLAT. setup/get-model.sh renames on the way in and
# tests/test_fetchmodel.py carries the case.
MODEL_SOURCE=google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf gemma-4-26B_q4_0-it gemma-4-26B-it-mmproj
# THE DRAFT MODEL COMES FROM A SECOND REPOSITORY and this field holds one, so
# it is named here instead: google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-assistant, file
# gemma-4-26b-A4B-it-assistant-Q4_0.gguf, 0.30 GiB, downloaded 04.09.2026.
# What was verified before it was wired up, out of the GGUF header:
#   general.architecture      gemma4-assistant   (present in libllama.so of
#                                                 build-vulkan AND the ROCm
#                                                 pin — checked with strings)
#   block_count               4
#   nextn_predict_layers      4
#   embedding_length_out      2816  ==  the served model's gemma4.embedding_length
#   token_embd.weight         present  (the seconds-long check defects.json ->
#                                       qwen36-sidecar-mtp-head-has-no-embeddings
#                                       exists for)
#   tokenizer.chat_template   present in BOTH files — the fallback-template
#                             trap of 04.09. (glm47flash) does not apply here
# MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN — MEASURED 04.09.2026 by the two-point method: load at
# two windows and take the GTT difference, so the weights and compute buffers
# cancel out of the subtraction instead of having to be estimated. Behind
# bench/sideserver.py on build-vulkan, in the shape this profile serves:
#
#     -c  65536   GTT (own, per DRM client) 17.73 GiB   RssAnon 0.24
#     -c 262144   GTT                       22.23 GiB   RssAnon 0.24
#
#     4.50 GiB over 196,608 tokens = 24.00 KiB per token
#
# THE BASE COMPUTED FROM EACH POINT INDEPENDENTLY AGREES EXACTLY: 17.73 - 1.50
# = 16.23 and 22.23 - 6.00 = 16.23. That agreement is the check, not a
# coincidence — it says the model is linear in the window and that neither
# point was taken in a disturbed state.
#
# THE VALUE IT REPLACED WAS 10.4 AND CAME FROM A NOTE, WHICH MODEL_KV_SOURCE
# SAID ITSELF. The note read "210 KiB/token on the 5 global layers of 30", and
# both halves are wrong. The GGUF says the layers are inverted from what that
# assumed:
#
#     global   5 layers   2 KV heads   512+512    full context     20.00 KiB/tok
#     sliding 25 layers   8 KV heads   256+256    1024 cells      200.00 KiB/tok
#
# The GLOBAL layers are the cheap ones. The 210 is roughly what the OTHER 25
# cost, and they are the ones a window keeps short.
#
# WHAT THE DRAFT MODEL COSTS, isolated from a second two-point pair the same
# hour (21.97 KiB/token and base 15.67 without it):
#
#     +2.03 KiB per token and +0.56 GiB of base
#
# AND THAT IS THE FINDING RATHER THAN A FOOTNOTE. GLM-4.7-Flash's MTP head
# measured 55.0 against 110.0 KiB/token on this same machine seven hours
# earlier — EXACTLY DOUBLE, because llama.cpp's MTP draft path allocates a
# second full-size KV cache. This one does not: common/speculative.cpp:1337
# documents three modes for draft-mtp, and the gemma4 one is `is_mem_shared` —
# "shares the target KV, runs all heads in one graph" — chosen when the draft
# context's ctx_other is the target context. A third point checked the model
# rather than merely repeating it: --swa-full AND the drafter together were
# PREDICTED at 30.06 GiB before the load and measured at 30.18, +0.4 %.
#
# FOR SCALE, all measured here: qwen36 21.0 KiB/token, flashnext 37.3,
# glm47flash 55.0, qwen38 74.3. This is the cheapest cache in the repo.
MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN=24.0
MODEL_KV_SOURCE=04.09.2026, two-point method behind bench/sideserver.py on build-vulkan, in this profile's serving shape (with the draft model): GTT 17.73 GiB at -c 65536 and 22.23 at -c 262144, i.e. 4.50 GiB over 196,608 tokens = 24.00 KiB per token, base 16.23 from both points independently. WITHOUT the draft model a second pair gave 21.97 and base 15.67, so the drafter costs +2.03 KiB/token and +0.56 GiB - NOT a second KV cache, unlike glm47flash's MTP head which exactly doubled its own. With --swa-full a third pair gave 224.00 KiB/token. Derivation from the GGUF config beforehand: 20.00 without --swa-full (9.9 % under) and 220.00 with (1.8 % under, the closest a derivation has come on this machine). Raw output in bench/reports/2026-09-04_1810_gemma26-memory/memory-observations.txt, analysis in ANALYSIS.md beside it.
# MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB — what GTT shows with the KV taken out: the weights the
# GPU really pins plus its compute buffers and the fixed 1024-cell windows of
# the 25 sliding layers. Computed from EACH of the two points above
# independently; they agree at 16.23.
#
# IT IS 1.67 GiB ABOVE the 14.56 GiB of file (13.45 main + 1.11 mmproj), which
# is the qwen36 pattern (0.80 above) rather than the glm47flash one (0.02
# below). 0.56 of the 1.67 is the draft model's own base; the rest is compute
# buffers and the sliding windows.
#
# DECLARING THIS AT ALL IS A COMMITMENT: a declared gtt_base REPLACES the file
# size in budget.plan() rather than being compared against it, so a placeholder
# here would silently under-charge the guard. That cost an hour on 04.09.2026.
# This value is measured; if LLAMA_BIN or the serving shape moves, re-measure
# it or delete the line.
MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB=16.23
# MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB — what stays resident OUTSIDE GTT. RssAnon of the RUNNING
# server, the one-line check this repo requires: 0.13-0.14 GiB without the
# drafter and 0.24 with it, over seven loads on 04.09.2026. The declared value
# is the largest.
#
# THE STANDING CAVEAT, and it has now been seen twice: these are SIDE-SERVER
# loads, without the gateway and without a warm -cram. A production shape reads
# more. qwen36 declares 0.29 from side servers, showed 0.38 at the switch, and
# read 1.27 on 04.09. after serving one sweep's reference cells. So this figure
# is a floor for the serving shape, not a ceiling.
MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB=0.24
# --- reasoning modes ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The rule and the vocabulary: setup/claude/modes.py.
#
# MEASURED 28.08.2026: this template (sha ae53464bf3be, shared by gemma26,
# gemma31 and batch) reads `enable_thinking` and IGNORES `reasoning_effort`
# completely — all six levels render byte-identically. So every level word
# means the same thing here, thinking on, and they share one prefix id rather
# than five. TEMPLATE_LEVELS=no-levels is that measurement and not an empty
# field: an absent line and an empty one read back identically, so silence
# would switch the load-time check off instead of narrowing it.
#
# ONE HALF OF THAT NOTE IS CONTRADICTED BY BEHAVIOUR MEASURED 04.09.2026, and
# both readings are kept here rather than one being quietly dropped. The note
# also said the template's DEFAULT is not to think — that unset and
# enable_thinking=false render the same prefix — and concluded there was no
# point in a `none` mode. What the model actually does, three requests one
# after another against one server, temperature 0, max_tokens 4096:
#
#     no chat_template_kwargs   4096 tokens, 56.0 s, ALL reasoning, NO answer
#     enable_thinking=false       32 tokens,  0.7 s, correct visible answer
#     enable_thinking=true      4096 tokens, 54.5 s, ALL reasoning, NO answer
#
# UNSET BEHAVES LIKE true, NOT LIKE false. Two byte-identical prefixes cannot
# produce that at temperature 0, so one of the two measurements is wrong about
# what it measured; the behavioural one is what a user experiences and is what
# the launch line below acts on. Re-running
# `python3 bench/suites/effort-vocabulary.py --profile gemma26` against
# /apply-template is what would settle which.
#
# This is also why every gemma26 cell of the 04.09. sweep carries speed.py's
# "the model answered in the thinking channel" warning at max_tokens=256: the
# sweep did not pass the flag, so the model reasoned past the budget in every
# copy cell. Those cells are decode rates and NOT copy-heavy ones.
MODES=low:on  medium:on  high:on  xhigh:on  max:on
TEMPLATE_LEVELS=no-levels
#
# --- the arguments, and what is and is not known about each -----------------
#
# -md + --spec-type draft-mtp: GOOGLE'S OWN DRAFT MODEL, AND IT IS THE REASON
#   THIS PROFILE IS RECOMMENDED. 0.30 GiB on disk, +0.8 GiB of GTT, and worth
#   this on `prose` — the workload no drafter can help, and the only column of
#   the 04.09. sweep with no spread warning in any of its seven cells:
#
#                        d512   d8192   d36k
#     vulkan-nospec      57.1    50.9    44.7
#     vulkan-assistant   91.5    65.9    49.8      <- this profile
#                        1.60x   1.29x   1.11x
#
#   ACCEPTANCE 85.5 / 73.3 / 67.0 %, AND THAT IS NOT WHY IT IS HERE. Yesterday
#   glm47flash's MTP head accepted 59-82 % and COST up to 42 % of throughput,
#   because it verified against a second full-size KV cache. Read acceptance
#   and t/s together or not at all; this one earns its place on the t/s column.
#
#   THE ROUTE IS NOT GUESSABLE FROM THE FLAG NAME. `--spec-type` has no
#   `gemma4-assistant` value — common/speculative.cpp lists eleven names and
#   that is not one of them. The assistant is driven by the draft-mtp
#   implementation, which carries three modes and picks the gemma one by
#   is_mem_shared. IF THE WIRING IS WRONG THE SERVER SAYS SO IN AS MANY WORDS:
#   `Gemma4Assistant requires ctx_other to be set` (src/llama-context.cpp:146).
#   That line did not appear in any load on 04.09.2026, and GTT rose by 0.69
#   GiB rather than by a second copy of the 13.45 GiB target — the two checks
#   together are what says the assistant loaded and not something else.
# --spec-draft-n-max 4: matches the assistant's own nextn_predict_layers 4,
#   read out of its GGUF. A real choice rather than a formality: the draft-mtp
#   driver only clamps n_max to the head count in its chain_heads mode, which
#   is NOT the mode this model uses.
# --spec-draft-p-min 0.75: the house value, carried so the two drafters were
#   compared on equal terms in the sweep. NOT itself measured on this model.
#
# NO --spec-type ngram-mod. THE N-GRAM DRAFTER WAS MEASURED AND REJECTED, which
#   is the opposite of the glm47flash result and of qwen36's:
#
#     prose, Vulkan       d512   d8192   d36k
#     nospec              57.1    50.9    44.7
#     ngram-mod           55.1    51.2    41.5     -3.5 % / +0.6 % / -7.2 %
#
#   It accepts 4-17 % on prose, which is the healthy direction — that drafter
#   drafts FROM THE PROMPT, so HIGH acceptance on novel text would mean the
#   model is repeating it — and it simply does not pay here.
#
#   NOTE WHAT THE ABSENCE OF THIS FLAG IS WORTH: --spec-type ACCUMULATES in
#   llama.cpp (common/arg.cpp uses push_back), so a later `--extra
#   "--spec-type ngram-mod"` would not REPLACE draft-mtp, it would append to
#   it. Two "control" loads came back byte-identical to the arms they were
#   controls for on 04.09. because of exactly that.
#
# BACKEND: VULKAN, AND MEASURED FOR THE FIRST TIME. The profile had served
#   build-vulkan since 17.08. on no measurement at all; it turns out to have
#   been right. Six cells, -c 65536, decode t/s on `prose`:
#
#                        d512   d8192   d36k
#     rocm-nospec        42.0    37.7    34.7
#     vulkan-nospec      57.1    50.9    44.7     Vulkan 1.36 / 1.35 / 1.29x
#     rocm-assistant     86.8    64.4    47.9
#     vulkan-assistant   91.5    65.9    49.8     Vulkan 1.05 / 1.02 / 1.04x
#
#   UNLIKE qwen36 THE ANSWER DOES NOT SPLIT. There ROCm took the win back as
#   soon as speculation was on; here Vulkan leads in all six cells. Same
#   direction as glm47flash, opposite to qwen38.
#
#   AND THE VULKAN ARM IS HANDICAPPED TWICE, both understating it: build-vulkan
#   is commit 54ee5ee of 22.08. against the ROCm arm's b10750-10, on a stack
#   where a build difference has been worth 25 %; and no RADV APU heap option
#   is set on this machine at all. So this margin is a floor.
#
# --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false}: NOT COSMETIC AND NOT
#   COPIED. Without it this model spends its whole token budget reasoning and
#   never reaches a visible answer — 4096 tokens and 56.0 s against 32 tokens
#   and 0.7 s, measured 04.09.2026 (see the reasoning-modes block above). MODES
#   turns thinking back on per request, which is what that mechanism is for.
#   The published Gemma 4 figures are thinking figures, so the quality question
#   and this flag are entangled the same way they are for glm47flash.
#
# NO --swa-full, AND THAT IS NOW A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A GAP. This is the
#   flag this profile was expected to gain, and the measurement said not to.
#   Four requests on the REAL Claude Code body (tools/synthetic.py), same head,
#   changed question — the shape setup/README.md's SWA section is about,
#   because Claude Code appends ~1624 tokens behind the question and this
#   model's window is 1024, so the divergence is always outside it:
#
#                          new     cache    reused    wall
#     without --swa-full   1043    13354    92.8 %    2.2 s
#     with    --swa-full    971    13426    93.3 %    1.8 s
#     (cold, either arm)  14397        0     0.0 %   17.6 s
#
#   72 TOKENS AND 0.4 SECONDS, FOR 24.96 GiB — --swa-full costs 224.00
#   KiB/token against 21.97, i.e. 43.4 GiB of GTT at -c 131072 against 19.2.
#   The work is already being done by -ctxcp/-cms below, which this profile has
#   carried since August.
#
#   WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY. The 100.2 s -> 10.4 s in setup/README.md was
#   measured on LAGUNA and is not contradicted — the MECHANISM transfers, the
#   MAGNITUDE plainly does not, and the difference is that laguna's profile has
#   no context checkpoints. It also does not say the reuse survives a
#   multi-turn conversation: this test repeats a single turn, and
#   setup/defects.json -> the-previous-answer-is-sometimes-not-reused describes
#   a checkpoint search that rejects every checkpoint newer than the common
#   prefix. Measuring that is open.
#
# -ctxcp 64 -cms 4096: THE REASON --swa-full IS NOT NEEDED, promoted from a
#   carried flag to a load-bearing one by the measurement above. 64 context
#   checkpoints at a minimum spacing of 4096 tokens; the changed question fell
#   back to a checkpoint 1043 tokens early rather than to the start.
#   The spacing itself is NOT tuned here — bench/suites/checkpoint-grid.py is
#   the instrument for that and has not been run on this model.
# -c 32768: A CAP, NOT A TUNING, AND IT IS THE ONE FLAG HERE THAT ANSWERS A
#   CORRECTNESS FINDING RATHER THAN A COST. This model loses anchors planted in
#   the MIDDLE of a long context, measured 04.09.2026 in four configurations
#   (see the depth-correctness block below); the shallowest depth at which it
#   was ever wrong is 35,843 on ROCm and 51,728 on the Vulkan build this
#   profile serves. 32768 sits below both, so a request cannot reach a depth
#   where this model has been observed to confabulate.
#
#   BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THAT IS AND IS NOT. Green at 4,070 / 19,958 / 35,843
#   is a MEASUREMENT, at three depths, twice each. "Therefore correct
#   everywhere below 32768" is an INFERENCE from those three points, not a
#   measurement — this suite plants anchors every 4,000 tokens and asks for
#   them, it does not sweep every depth. The cap makes the observed failures
#   unreachable; it does not make the model good at long context.
#
#   It costs almost nothing: 0.75 GiB of KV at 24.00 KiB/token, against 3.00
#   at -c 131072 and 6.00 at the model's declared 262144. Raise it only
#   together with a fresh depth-correctness run, and only if that run is
#   green — the window and the finding are one decision, not two.
# -np 1: NOT a fresh decision. Two independent defects were measured on this
#   hardware at two slots — the gfx1151 HIP race (llama.cpp #27579) and
#   slot-restore poisoning; the matrix is in setup/env/qwen38.env. It was
#   ABSENT from this profile until 04.09.2026, which meant the default and a
#   known-corrupting configuration.
# f16 KV: the profile carried `-ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0` until 04.09.2026 and it is
#   gone. This repo's objection to q8 KV is a CORRECTNESS objection and not a
#   speed one — it dropped qwen38's long-context task. At 24.00 KiB/token there
#   is nothing to buy with it.
# NO --kv-unified: removed 04.09.2026. It is in docs/HANDOVER.md under "do not
#   try again", together with -np 3.
# --port 8080: NOT A FREE CHOICE, and the profile served 8081 until 04.09.2026.
#   setup/switch-model.sh reads the port out of LLAMA_ARGS and aborts when it
#   disagrees with the gateway's — so this profile could not be switched to at
#   all, and would have failed its own preflight. bench/sweep.py polls /slots
#   on 8080 for the same reason.
# depth-correctness: THE FINDING THAT CAPS THIS PROFILE, AND THE ONE THING
#   HERE THAT IS ABOUT THE MODEL RATHER THAN ABOUT A FLAG. Anchors planted
#   every 4,000 tokens, each asked twice, at six depths to 99,398 — 48 answers
#   per run. FOUR configurations were run because the suite's own docstring
#   says a WRONG is not proof until the other backend has been tried:
#
#     configuration                      ok/48   first wrong at
#     Vulkan + assistant (this profile)   40      51,728
#     Vulkan + assistant + --swa-full     38      51,728
#     Vulkan, NO drafter at all           40      51,728
#     ROCm  + assistant                   36      35,843
#
#   EVERY ARM FAILS, AND THE TWO OBVIOUS SUSPECTS ARE EXONERATED. Removing the
#   drafter changes nothing (40/48 either way, same depths, same anchors);
#   --swa-full changes nothing and is one cell WORSE; ROCm is worse still and
#   fails a whole step earlier. So this is not speculation, not the sliding
#   window, and not the backend.
#
#   THE CONTROL WAS A PROFILE COPY, NOT AN --extra OVERRIDE, and that mattered:
#   --spec-type ACCUMULATES, so `--extra "--spec-type none"` would have left
#   draft-mtp running and produced a "control" that was not one. The copy's
#   argv names neither -md nor --spec-type at all.
#
#   WHAT MAKES IT DAMNING RATHER THAN MERELY BAD: the same suite, the same day,
#   the same machine, is 48/48 CLEAN on both alternatives — qwen36 (11:52) and
#   glm47flash (16:46). This is not a hard test that everything fails; it is a
#   test that only this model fails.
#
#   THE FAILING ANCHOR IS ALMOST ALWAYS `middle`, and `first` is never wrong.
#   The model keeps the start of the context and the end and loses the part in
#   between — which is what 5 global layers of 30 would predict, though nothing
#   here establishes that as the cause.
#
#   NOT SEPARABLE FROM THE QUANT. This is a QAT-q4_0 and no second quant of
#   this model is on the disk, so "the model" and "this file" cannot be told
#   apart. That the provenance is Google's own QAT rather than a third-party
#   imatrix-less quant makes it LESS likely to be the file, not more.
#
#   Reports: bench/reports/2026-09-04_1902_depth-correctness_gemma26-vulkan-
#   assistant, …_1918_…-swafull, …_1923_…-NOSPEC, …_1931_…-ROCM-assistant.
#
# -fa on, -fit off, -ub 512, -b 2048, -cram 4096, --mmproj: carried. -fit off
#   is a refusal to let the fitter guess (flashnext.env carries the crash
#   report it comes from); -ub 512 is about this MACHINE rather than this model
#   (prompt-cache reuse is quantized to the ubatch, measured twice here) and is
#   NOT re-confirmed in this serving shape; -cram 4096 was computed in August
#   against the sliding window and its reasoning is unchanged by anything
#   measured today.
LLAMA_ARGS=--alias gemma26 -m @MODELS@/gemma-4-26B_q4_0-it.gguf --mmproj @MODELS@/gemma-4-26B-it-mmproj.gguf \
  -md @MODELS@/gemma-4-26b-A4B-it-assistant-Q4_0.gguf \
  --spec-type draft-mtp --spec-draft-n-max 4 --spec-draft-p-min 0.75 \
  -ngl 999 -fa on -fit off -ub 512 -b 2048 -c 32768 -np 1 \
  -cram 4096 -ctxcp 64 -cms 4096 \
  --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false} \
  --jinja --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080

# Backend for this profile. MEASURED 04.09.2026 for the first time
# (bench/reports/2026-09-04_1823_sweep_gemma26): Vulkan wins all six cells,
# and unlike qwen36 the answer does not split once speculation is on. The
# choice is MODEL-dependent on this machine and has flipped in both directions
# before, so it stays a per-profile line rather than a default.
LLAMA_BIN=llama.cpp/build-vulkan/bin/llama-server
#
# --- open points, in the order they block something -------------------------
#
#   [x] DOES GOOGLE'S DRAFT MODEL WORK — yes. No `Gemma4Assistant requires
#       ctx_other` in any load, GTT +0.69 GiB rather than +13.45, acceptance
#       67-86 %, and 1.11-1.60x on prose. The lever that decided qwen36's day
#       for 0.3 GiB instead of 17.
#   [x] MEMORY — three two-point pairs plus one prediction checked against a
#       seventh load. 24.00 KiB/token in this shape, and the drafter does NOT
#       double the cache.
#   [x] BACKEND — Vulkan, all six cells, not split.
#   [x] SPECULATION — Google's drafter in, the n-gram drafter OUT (-3.5 to
#       -7.2 % on prose).
#   [x] --swa-full — measured and REJECTED: 0.4 s for 24.96 GiB.
#   [x] THINKING — must be switched off explicitly or the model never reaches
#       an answer.
#   [x] SPEED AGAINST PRODUCTION, same sweep, same hour: prose 91.5/65.9/49.8
#       against qwen36's 42.7/39.0/32.9 — 2.14 / 1.69 / 1.51x, at 19.2 GiB of
#       GTT against 27.8.
#   [!] depth-correctness — FAILS, and it is the finding of the day. 40/48 in
#       this profile's shape, first wrong at 51,728 tokens, in all FOUR
#       configurations tried; qwen36 and glm47flash are 48/48 on the same
#       suite the same day. The window is capped at 32768 in response. See the
#       depth-correctness block above and setup/defects.json ->
#       `gemma26-loses-the-middle-of-a-long-context`.
#
#   [ ] STILL OPEN, and none of it blocks the verdict:
#       * `count` AND `copy` ARE NOT COMPARABLE ACROSS THE TWO MODELS and the
#         gap is large: qwen36 reads 213-282 t/s there against this model's
#         47-67. Every gemma26 cell in those columns carries the thinking-
#         channel warning, so they are decode rates and not copy rates — the
#         sweep ran before the enable_thinking finding. Re-running the sweep
